  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Securing Connectivity For Jaguars Along Expanding Highways of the Sky Islands</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to secure habitat connectivity in the Sky Islands region by coordinating with local stakeholders, road ecology experts and agency officials to advance in the establishment of wildlife crossings along key highways of Sonora. The project is intended to conserve jaguars (Panther onca) by preventing deaths caused by collisions with vehicles and by maintaining habitat connectivity for their expansion northward of their current known range. Habitat connectivity is threatened by the present expansion of highways in the region. Specific activities include (1) training of local agency staff, land-managers and contractors in skills necessary to monitor, prevent and reduce the impact of highways on wildlife; (2) issuing recommendations to Transport Authorities who are expecting them, so they can be used as plans for the establishment of wildlife crossings; (3) informing the regional population on the need for habitat connectivity and the purpose of wildlife crossings; (4) beginning wildlife monitoring along drainages and mitigating infrastructures to understand how wildlife uses them; and (5) collaborating with Environmental Consultants hired by Transportation Authorities in drafting an Environmental Impact Statement that ensures a new highway bypass between the cities of muris and Cananea, has a minimal impact on the habitat connectivity needed by jaguars.</narrative>
  </description>
